      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students who received at least one A in history or math, and then subtract this from the total number of students to find the number of students who did not receive an A in either course.\n\nFirst, let's define the variables:\n- Let \\( H \\) be the set of students who received an A in history.\n- Let \\( M \\) be the set of students who received an A in math.\n- The total number of students is \\( |U| = 30 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in history is \\( |H| = 7 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in math is \\( |M| = 13 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in both history and math is \\( |H \\cap M| = 4 \\).\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received at least one A in history or math, which is \\( |H \\cup M| \\). According to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\nSo, 16 students received at least one A in history or math. Therefore, the number of students who did not receive an A in either course is:\n\\[\n30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\nThus, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
