      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following sets:\n\n- Let \\( H \\) be the set of students who received an A in history.\n- Let \\( M \\) be the set of students who received an A in math.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- The total number of students is 30.\n- The number of students who received an A in history, \\( |H| \\), is 7.\n- The number of students who received an A in math, \\( |M| \\), is 13.\n- The number of students who received an A in both courses, \\( |H \\cap M| \\), is 4.\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received an A in at least one of the two courses, which is \\( |H \\cup M| \\). According to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nSo, 16 students received an A in at least one of the two courses. To find the number of students who did not receive an A in any of the two courses, we subtract the number of students who received an A in at least one of the courses from the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
