      "text": "Let's break down the problem step by step and use Python code to ensure accuracy in our calculations.\n\n1. **Understand the Problem:**\n   - There are 30 students in total.\n   - Seven students received an A in history.\n   - 13 students received an A in math.\n   - Four students received an A in both history and math.\n   - We need to find out how many students did not receive an A in either history or math.\n\n2. **Use the principle of inclusion and exclusion:**\n   The number of students who received an A in either history or math (or both) is given by:\n   \\[\n   |H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n   \\]\n   where \\(|H|\\) is the number of students who received an A in history, \\(|M|\\) is the number of students who received an A in math, and \\(|H \\cap M|\\) is the number of students who received an A in both history and math.\n\n   Plugging in the given values:\n   \\[\n   |H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n   \\]\n   So, 16 students received an A in either history or math (or both).\n\n3. **Calculate the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses:**\n   The total number of students is 30. The number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is:\n   \\[\n   30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n   \\]\n\nNow, let's verify this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given data\ntotal_students = 30\nA_in_history = 7\nA_in_math = 13\nA_in_both = 4\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\nA_in_either_or_both = A_in_history + A_in_math - A_in_both\n\n# Students who did not receive an A in either course\nstudents_without_A = total_students - A_in_either_or_both\nprint(students_without_A)\n```\n```output\n14\n```\nThe number of students who did not receive an A in either history or math is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
